    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28197",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/795ec06d95058de55d870db3a7559f44277003afdfd5ce1231493abd520404e2.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
